\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

% --- P1: Scale of the problem and why it's hard ---
Predictive maintenance of turbofan engines underpins a trillion-dollar aerospace industry and shares structural challenges with health monitoring in automotive, medical devices or defence
~\cite{Ucar_Karakose_Kirimca_2024, Tiddens_Braaksma_Tinga_2022, Arena_Collotta_Luca_Ruggieri_Termine_2021, Khan_Sohaib_Rashid_Ali_Akbar_Basit_Ahmad_2021}: an operator must infer the true internal health state of a complex physical system from a limited set of indirect, noisy sensor measurements.
In a commercial turbofan, this health state encodes the efficiency and flow capacity of five mechanical subsystems (fan, booster, compressors, turbines) evolving through fouling, erosion, thermal fatigue, and discrete damage events over thousands of flight cycles. None of these state variables are directly measurable in service. What operators observe instead is a small number of sensor channels (temperatures, pressures, shaft speeds, fuel flow) recorded across fundamentally different operating conditions: cruise at altitude, takeoff at sea level, climb through intermediate regimes. The same health state produces distinct sensor signatures depending on flight phase and ambient conditions, while different state configurations can appear indistinguishable within a single operating context. This makes health state recovery a structurally underdetermined problem: with seven sensor channels mapping to ten underlying state dimensions, the system is inherently sub-determined even before
accounting for sensor noise and context confounding.
The field has increasingly turned to data-driven methods to tackle this problem~\cite{Nguyen_Medjaher_Tran_2023, Davari_Veloso_Costa_Pereira_Ribeiro_Gama_2021, Biggio_Kastanis_2020}, but their quality is
bounded by the benchmarks on which they are developed.

% --- P2: CMAPSS critique ---
For over a decade, turbofan prognostics research has been dominated by the
Commercial Modular Aero-Propulsion System Simulation
(CMAPSS)~\cite{Saxena_Goebel_Simon_Eklund_2008} and its successor
N-CMAPSS~\cite{Arias_Chao_Kulkarni_Goebel_Fink_2021, Arias_Chao_Kulkarni_Goebel_Fink_2022}.
These datasets model engines that degrade monotonically from a healthy initial state until failure, with no maintenance interventions.
The evaluation task is almost exclusively remaining-useful-life (RUL) regression: predict, from a window of sensor readings, how many cycles remain before the engine crosses a fixed failure
threshold~\cite{Ramasso_Saxena, De_Pater_Mitici_2022, Kim_Park_Lee_Ko_Hwang_2025, Costa_Sanchez_2022}.
While CMAPSS has been invaluable for establishing the field, its limitations are well documented: degradation follows a biphasic monotonic then exponential trajectory that can be predicted by trend extrapolation alone \cite{thil2025glide}; models never need to reason about state resets, imperfect repairs, or complex scheduling logic; sensor observations lack structured multi-context design; and the ground-truth health state is reduced to a scalar RUL label rather than a full state vector, precluding evaluation of whether a learned representation captures the \emph{structure} of engine health. N-CMAPSS introduced more contexts and underlying states but retained the run-to-failure paradigm: engines still degrade without intervention, and evaluation remains scalar RUL prediction.

% --- P3: What the real world looks like (the gap) ---
Real turbofan fleets do not operate this way. Commercial engines are maintained under stringent airworthiness regulations that impose life-limiting-part (LLP) cycle ceilings \cite{iata, eCFR, immarigeon2003life, Guo_Sun_Li_Chen_Hou_Yang_2017}, mandate inspections, and trigger opportunistic component bundling to minimise costly shop visits \cite{riegler2007geared, vasigh2012aircraft}.
Maintenance is imperfect: newly installed parts enter ageing engine environments, restoration quality varies across workshops, and inspection findings (components whose degradation was missed) force unplanned emergency returns.
The resulting fleet behaviour is one where engines are kept within a narrow healthy operating band by active management, with degradation unfolding not as a single monotonic arc but as a sequence of sawtooth cycles punctuated by partial resets of varying magnitude, overlaid with rare stochastic events (bird strikes, compressor surges, icing, sensor faults) that create discontinuities in either the health state, the observations, or both. No existing public benchmark captures this setting. 

% additions:
There is a general need for new dataset for turbofan engines \cite{Stanton_Munir_Ikram_El_Bakry_2023}

% --- P4: TurboSens introduction ---
We introduce \textbf{TurboSens}, a large-scale synthetic turbofan
degradation dataset generated by OpenDeckSMR \cite{psaropoulos_2025_17804895}, a high-fidelity
thermodynamic engine simulator, designed to close this gap.
TurboSens models a \emph{well-maintained fleet}: engines degrade through
heterogeneous degradation-mode archetypes (compressor-driven, fan-driven,
turbine-driven, balanced), are maintained by an expert policy combining
LLP ceilings, health-correlated scheduling, and opportunistic bundling (min-build) with imperfect restoration and stochastic inspection findings. Ten categories of rare stochastic events enrich trajectories with consequential disturbances encountered in real operations. Sensor observations are recorded across twelve structured flight contexts per timestep under a correlated weather process, providing the multi-context diagnostic information that operators rely on in practice.
Crucially, the simulator exposes the \emph{full ten-dimensional ground-truth health state} at every timestep alongside the observations, enabling what we call an \emph{inverse evaluation protocol}: models are trained in a fully self-supervised manner on sensor sequences alone, then their learned representations are evaluated against the known health state through probing, forecasting, and anomaly detection tasks. Because the true state is available but never used as a training signal, TurboSens supports rigorous evaluation of whether self-supervised representations capture the causal structure of engine health, going well beyond scalar RUL regression.

% --- P5: Why the benchmark is both hard and informative ---
The well-maintained fleet setting makes TurboSens a demanding benchmark.
Because the expert policy keeps engines in good condition, a model must
resolve genuinely small state differences from sensor signatures that
vary subtly across neighbouring health configurations, rather than
merely tracking a monotonic decline toward failure.
At the same time, this is precisely what makes the dataset valuable:
maintenance resets, stochastic events, and the asymmetric
inter-maintenance interval distribution provide rich structure for
models to discover, and the sub-determined observation model (seven
sensors, ten state dimensions, multiple flight contexts) rewards
architectures that learn to aggregate evidence across operating
conditions.

% --- P6: Contributions ---
\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{TurboSens}, a large-scale turbofan degradation dataset
 with paired ground-truth health states, featuring heterogeneous degradation archetypes, a realistic multi-mechanism maintenance policy with imperfect restoration and inspection findings, ten categories of stochastic events, multi-context sensor observations under a correlated weather process, and structured metadata enabling fine-grained analysis and full customization.

\item An \textbf{online interactive environment} built on OpenDeckSMR that allows agents to query engine state, select from multiple discrete maintenance actions with realistic restoration dynamics, and generate new trajectories on demand, enabling model predictive control of possible outcomes, reinforcement learning, and active research beyond offline replay.

\item An \textbf{inverse evaluation protocol} exploiting full ground-truth access to the ten-dimensional health state: models are trained self-supervised on sensor sequences, then their representations are directly evaluated against the true state through health-state probing, degradation velocity estimation, multi-step latent forecasting, and out-of-distribution detection.

\item A \textbf{safety‑aware evaluation lens}: the maintenance logic mirrors regulatory cycle limits and inspection protocols, allowing explicit measurement of whether a world model respects real‑world operational boundaries, a step toward evaluating system alignment.

\end{enumerate}



\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Turbofan degradation datasets and PHM benchmarks.}
The C-MAPSS benchmark~\cite{Saxena_Goebel_Simon_Eklund_2008}, introduced for the PHM08
challenge, established the run-to-failure paradigm that still dominates turbofan prognostics: engines degrade monotonically from a nominal state to a fixed failure threshold with no maintenance, no repair, and a scalar remaining-useful-life (RUL) label as the sole evaluation target.
A review by the dataset's creators~\cite{Ramasso_Saxena} acknowledges the resulting limitations, including the absence of ground-truth state labels, a simplified degradation model, and a narrow range of failure modes. N-CMAPSS~\cite{Arias_Chao_Kulkarni_Goebel_Fink_2021} improved on CMAPSS by introducing realistic flight profiles and richer sensor streams via a thermodynamic simulator, but retains the run-to-failure paradigm with no maintenance and scalar RUL evaluation.

\paragraph{Interactive simulators and agentic environments.}
Gym-style interactive environments are the standard testbed for
reinforcement learning and model predictive control~\cite{towers2024gymnasium, 
todorov2012mujoco}, but industrial degradation settings have received
comparatively little attention in this form.
\citet{Maulana_Starr_Ompusunggu_2023} highlight the need to move beyond reactive maintenance toward policies reasoning over longer action horizons, a capability that requires an online simulator rather than a static dataset.
No existing turbofan benchmark offers this: an agent cannot apply a
maintenance action and observe the consequence.
TurboSens exposes multiple discrete maintenance actions with realistic cost structures and imperfect restoration dynamics, supports on-demand trajectory generation for counterfactual policy evaluation, and allows stress-testing under out-of-distribution degradation regimes generated live by the simulator.

\paragraph{World models and self-supervised learning on sensor sequences.}
Joint-embedding predictive architectures~\cite{Balestriero_LeCun_2025, Assran_Bardes_Fan_Garrido_Howes_Mojtaba_Komeili_Muckley_Rizvi_Roberts_et_al_2025} and recurrent world models~\cite{okada2021dreaming, okada2022dreamingv2, Maes_Lidec_Scieur_LeCun_Balestriero_2026} are increasingly proposed as general-purpose representations for complex dynamical systems, trained to predict future states in a learned latent space from
high-dimensional observations.
A growing body of work applies similar self-supervised objectives to industrial sensor sequences~\cite{wang2020data, krishnan2022self, deng2024enhancing, calabrese2021predictive}, motivated by label scarcity in real deployments.
A shared limitation across both communities is evaluation: without
ground-truth access to the true system state, it is unclear whether a
learned representation captures physical structure or merely statistical
regularities, and evaluation collapses onto downstream task return or
reconstruction quality. TurboSens is designed to make this question answerable. Because OpenDeckSMR exposes the true ten-dimensional health state at every timestep, any world model or SSL encoder trained on sensor sequences can be evaluated directly against the ground truth through an \emph{inverse evaluation protocol}: train without labels, then probe the frozen representation against the true state via regression, forecasting, and anomaly detection tasks.
The industrial setting adds structure that canonical benchmarks lack: a sub-determined observation model (seven sensors for ten state dimensions across twelve flight contexts), maintenance-induced state discontinuities, and stochastic events that test whether a model's surprise signal identifies anomalous regimes unseen during training.

\paragraph{Physics-understanding benchmarks for world models.}
A growing body of work evaluates whether self-supervised world models acquire physical understanding from high-dimensional observations. Object-level benchmarks such as Physion~\cite{bear1physion} test rigid-body outcome prediction on synthetic scenes, and CLEVRER~\cite{yiclevrer} probes causal reasoning over collision videos. IntPhys~\cite{riochet2018intphys} and its successor IntPhys2~\cite{Bordes_Garrido_Kao_Williams_Rabbat_Dupoux_2025} adopt a \emph{violation of expectation} paradigm, scoring models on their ability to discriminate physically possible from impossible scenes across principles such as object permanence, immutability, and solidity. Two structural limitations run through this line of work. First, reducing physical understanding to binary discrimination effectively reframes the problem as out-of-distribution detection: a model must flag that something unusual has happened, not represent what should have happened instead. Second, real physical systems admit multiple plausible futures whose likelihoods depend on interventions and latent state, yet evaluation of generative world models such as V-JEPA~\cite{Assran_Bardes_Fan_Garrido_Howes_Mojtaba_Komeili_Muckley_Rizvi_Roberts_et_al_2025} and Dreamer~\cite{okada2021dreaming, okada2022dreamingv2} collapses onto downstream task return or reconstruction quality, neither of which measures whether the full distribution of outcomes is captured.
TurboSens supports a richer evaluation: the simulator can be queried online under arbitrary action sequences and exposes the full ground-truth state, enabling counterfactual trajectory generation, direct comparison of predictive distributions against the simulator's true distribution over futures, and treatment of physics-breaking regimes (accelerated degradation, correlated failure) as one axis among several rather than the sole evaluation target.

