\paragraph{System model.}
A turbofan engine is modeled as a discrete‑time dynamical system with a latent
health state $\mathbf{s}_t \in \mathbb{R}^K$ ($K{=}10$).
The state vector encodes efficiency and flow‑capacity deviations for five
subsystems: booster, fan, high‑pressure compressor (HPC), high‑pressure turbine
(HPT), and low‑pressure turbine (LPT).
Degradation follows archetype‑specific stochastic dynamics
(Appendix~\ref{sec:dataset_degradation}), and discrete maintenance actions
$a_t \in \mathcal{A}$ partially restore the affected components.

\paragraph{Observations.}
At each timestep, $S{=}7$ sensors are sampled under $C$ flight contexts,
yielding an observation tensor $\mathbf{o}_t \in \mathbb{R}^{S \times C}$.
The mapping $\mathbf{o}_t = g(\mathbf{s}_t, \phi) + \epsilon$ is nonlinear and
context‑dependent; different operating regimes excite different parts of the
thermodynamic cycle, so the full multi‑context tensor carries richer
diagnostic signal than any single slice.
(For TurboSens1, $C{=}4$; for TurboSens3, $C{=}12$.)

\paragraph{Evaluation task.}
Given a sequence of observations and actions, we ask whether a world model
trained solely to predict future observations learns latent representations
that recover the true health state $\mathbf{s}_t$.
We probe a frozen encoder by training a small MLP to regress $\mathbf{s}_t$
and measure per‑component $R^2$ on held‑out episodes.
We also probe temporal structure via a steps‑to‑next‑action regression task
(Section~\ref{sec:evaluation}).


